Criteria recorded before opening native.md

Sources: normative spec §§2.4,4.1,4.3–4.8,6.7,9.2,9.4–9.7,10.3,11.4/7/8,Appendix B; accepted syntax; pre-adoption runtime and multicore; records §§2/6, value-types §4.6, types-joint J2/J6, lazy-reentry §7a, host-concurrency D/H rules; standalone/no-library-exceptions principles.

1. A resource wrapper is nominal data, not authority by integer equality alone. Hidden constructors must actually prevent fabrication. Audit field reads, constructor aliases/updates, inspect/hash, structural record views, exported type aliases and value-type extraction, host service acquisition, service replacement, user @host/@external declarations, namespace collisions and generic forwarding. Observing a number and injecting it are separate problems.
2. Any new restriction must be a precise general language rule, usable outside IO (three unrelated uses), independent of library path and inference order. State exactly which expression is rejected, whether aliases preserve authority, and why principal types survive. Legal fakes and forwarding should remain expressible.
3. One worker and N workers implement some legal sequential order of observable task steps. No observable worker identities in equality/hash/inspect, results, errors, seeds or handles. Exact capture semantics and keep_env must preserve services after resume and in finals. Transport preserves identity, cycles and sharing; started tasks and requests stay home.
4. Single-owner Store and request tables; one root continuation per worker; no lost work/wakeup, duplicate start, double commit, cross-run confusion, idle false deadlock or blocked reactor. Atomic scheduler operations cannot call arbitrary i code or suspend while holding a shared lock. Nested runs keep their own request roots and must yield only the outer calling task.
5. Explicit resource lifecycle: open pending, host-ready/provisional, decoded-unclaimed, claimed, closing, closed. Define cancellation and stop at every edge, races with close and blocked read/accept/wait, lease release, id reuse, wrong-kind IDs, stale callbacks. Stop runs no i finals yet host cleanup completes without deadlock. Detached syscalls cannot retain Store/run or consume another run's input. Two runs in one host cannot access/close each other's resources, including reused integer IDs.
6. Reusable host instances vs run-owned resources: sequential exports, tests, nested task.run, multiple concurrent runs and callbacks must have unambiguous owners and cleanup boundaries. Status 0, host failures, traps and future-drop must agree.
7. runtime and multicore must supply one implementable set of APIs, representation, portability, scheduling/transport, target and teardown rules. Replacement spec text, examples, checklists and tests must agree, including those not edited. R1/R2/IO may not lock in a representation or primitive surface that native immediately replaces. Check every claimed compiler seam against code; distinguish a planned feature from an infeasible implementation claim.

Hard programs/traces: leak File.id or inspect it; acquire raw service through environment parameter; copy raw fields through generic callbacks; substitute a fake raw open that returns a guessed ID; bind real raw service captured from entry; declare equivalent raw namespace; pass Int through aliases/records/type extraction; copy socket across go; close on worker A while B awaits IO; cancellation between host creation, resume decode and take; panic in worker A while B loops or owns provisional reply; destroy/run twice in one host; nested run awaiting an outer request; scope close while other worker has registered wait; start-up values/identity duplicated across Stores; pure yielding task beside pending IO; simultaneous CML commits and cancellation.
